1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:06,596
Team MedVerse presents MedVerse-Agent: policy-grounded institutional coordination for a
trustworthy medical metaverse.

2
00:00:06,739 --> 00:00:11,617
One patient-related event can involve five institutions, different evidence, and
different authority.

3
00:00:11,759 --> 00:00:15,500
The challenge is to coordinate the workflow without transferring that authority.

4
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:22,181
The patient Digital Twin maintains the observations actually supplied, their timestamps,
and their sources.

5
00:00:22,339 --> 00:00:28,798
It separates clinical values from evidence quality, including freshness, integrity, and
missing information.

6
00:00:28,956 --> 00:00:34,248
The Hospital role can propose verification and professional review; Cyber assesses
technical trust.

7
00:00:34,406 --> 00:00:39,500
This is an observed representation, not a complete physiological simulation or a
diagnosis.

8
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:42,913
Three evidence layers remain distinct.

9
00:00:43,079 --> 00:00:45,897
Modeled readings describe an individual scenario.

10
00:00:46,064 --> 00:00:51,336
Korean cancer-incidence statistics provide population context, not patient facts.

11
00:00:51,502 --> 00:00:55,104
Korean policy records inform institutional permissions.

12
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:59,500
The NCC registry remains, but its optional CSV is absent here.

13
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:05,041
From the verified project folder, launch the presenter sandbox in your Python
environment.

14
00:01:05,189 --> 00:01:09,500
For this recording, I explicitly select the BM25 lexical backend.

15
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:12,409
This is the actual server starting.

16
00:01:12,559 --> 00:01:18,878
It provides a commitment workspace, the original clinical and Digital Twin workspace,
and a read-only presentation gallery.

17
00:01:19,028 --> 00:01:21,162
The local address is shown in the terminal.

18
00:01:21,312 --> 00:01:23,500
No real hospital or device is connected.

19
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:28,677
The interface separates the five institutional roles from the harmonization coordinator.

20
00:01:28,826 --> 00:01:33,500
Events are released progressively; the controller cannot inspect future proposals.

21
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:40,083
The presentation gallery explains the original sensor-state components with an existing
modeled fixture.

22
00:01:40,246 --> 00:01:48,433
A low oxygen-saturation reading and raised heart rate produce a proposal to verify
measurements, contact the patient, and request human review.

23
00:01:48,596 --> 00:01:55,325
Later network and identity findings affect confidence in the evidence, not the supplied
physiological values.

24
00:01:55,488 --> 00:01:59,500
This is a labelled component replay, not live sensor acquisition.

25
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:05,747
Now open the original clinical and Digital Twin workspace and release the available
context.

26
00:02:05,913 --> 00:02:12,350
Its observation-aware adapter does not invent vital signs when an event contains only a
concern or an action contract.

27
00:02:12,515 --> 00:02:17,086
Unverified describes the evidence stream. It does not mean the patient is well.

28
00:02:17,252 --> 00:02:19,500
This remains a separate modeled session.

29
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:23,748
In P zero one, choose Prompt and MedVerse commitment.

30
00:02:23,918 --> 00:02:30,524
Event one exposes a pending institutional review, a bounded waiting reason, and an
authorized preparation.

31
00:02:30,693 --> 00:02:33,310
The waiting start and expiry are visible.

32
00:02:33,479 --> 00:02:41,500
Preparation does not count as clinical review or approval, and an eligible proposal
without satisfied prerequisites produces no effect.

33
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:44,207
Compare Cyber and Hospital.

34
00:02:44,359 --> 00:02:48,500
Each shows its own released contracts, not unrestricted peer information.

35
00:02:48,651 --> 00:02:50,236
This is an operator view.

36
00:02:50,388 --> 00:02:55,500
Live proposal inputs are fixed contracts, not newly generated LLM recommendations.

37
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:59,026
Event two supplies the authorized alternative.

38
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:04,530
The coordinator combines the compatible institutional actions and revalidates them
before commitment.

39
00:03:04,684 --> 00:03:11,272
Open the receipt to inspect the actual proposal, institutional owner, current authority
checks, and modeled effect.

40
00:03:11,426 --> 00:03:13,467
Export the released-event trace.

41
00:03:13,621 --> 00:03:17,500
The receipt distinguishes a selected plan from an action that actually committed.

42
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:22,342
P zero four shows why waiting cannot be a universal solution.

43
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:27,510
With eager commitment, the research action uses an earlier valid authorization.

44
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:32,990
With MedVerse waiting and no replacement, that authorization is revoked before
commitment.

45
00:03:33,174 --> 00:03:38,373
The later request is correctly withheld, but the earlier opportunity has been lost.

46
00:03:38,556 --> 00:03:43,500
Previously valid effects are not retrospectively erased or called unauthorized.

47
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:47,209
P zero seven isolates the Regulator role.

48
00:03:47,377 --> 00:03:52,979
Initially, its oversight action cannot proceed because the required modeled authority is
missing.

49
00:03:53,147 --> 00:03:57,079
Only after the approval event can that institution-owned action commit.

50
00:03:57,246 --> 00:04:03,500
The coordinator never manufactures the approval, and the Regulator agent is not an
actual government authority.

51
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:08,593
For a final boundary challenge, pause a plan before commitment.

52
00:04:08,777 --> 00:04:12,229
Change its modeled authority and then attempt to commit it.

53
00:04:12,413 --> 00:04:18,141
The execution boundary rejects the stale authorization, and no new modeled effect
occurs.

54
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:21,307
The rejected receipt remains inspectable.

55
00:04:21,491 --> 00:04:27,500
Permission is checked against the current context, not merely remembered from an earlier
proposal.

56
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:35,501
The evidence page filters source eligibility and recorded dates, Korean jurisdiction,
and institutional access before ranking.

57
00:04:35,651 --> 00:04:41,440
Here, a Korean research query returns source-linked records through the explicitly
selected BM25 backend.

58
00:04:41,590 --> 00:04:45,500
Relevance supports reasoning; it does not independently authorize an action.

59
00:04:46,450 --> 00:04:49,147
The same interface accepts an English query.

60
00:04:49,299 --> 00:04:52,402
Now request an unknown exact policy identifier.

61
00:04:52,555 --> 00:04:55,117
No nearby article is silently substituted.

62
00:04:55,269 --> 00:05:03,500
The dense option uses normalized, 384-dimensional multilingual embeddings, but this
recording does not relabel lexical results as semantic retrieval.

63
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:08,591
The gallery now shows archived measurements, not newly executed tests.

64
00:05:08,756 --> 00:05:14,195
This Digital Twin diagnostic shows why a new consent update must not make old telemetry
appear fresh.

65
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:17,500
Observation provenance remains field-specific.

66
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:22,103
The original architecture comparison retains B one through B five.

67
00:05:22,256 --> 00:05:31,301
On seventy modeled variants from seven archetypes, policy filtering alone achieved
coordination quality of zero point six; the full reference architecture achieved one.

68
00:05:31,454 --> 00:05:35,500
This is bounded architectural evidence, not universal superiority.

69
00:05:36,450 --> 00:05:39,651
The later commitment study addresses a different question.

70
00:05:39,826 --> 00:05:45,463
MedVerse completed fifteen of thirty-two branches, compared with fourteen for eager
commitment.

71
00:05:45,637 --> 00:05:50,500
It gained four branches, lost three, and tied the capable bounded-wait control.

72
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:57,105
These plots show each institution's decisions, preparations, commitments, and
escalation.

73
00:05:57,273 --> 00:06:01,055
They are correlated behavior counts, not predictive accuracy.

74
00:06:01,222 --> 00:06:04,500
Each contribution links to actual workflow receipts.

75
00:06:05,450 --> 00:06:10,083
Architecture, retrieval, and commitment use different benchmarks and denominators.

76
00:06:10,236 --> 00:06:17,939
The successful Windows dense run is reported separately: Recall at three was ninety-five
percent in English and seventy-five percent in Korean.

77
00:06:18,092 --> 00:06:20,500
Language sensitivity remains visible.

78
00:06:21,450 --> 00:06:25,543
Reusable coordination still needs domain-specific mandates and sources.

79
00:06:25,697 --> 00:06:30,500
The report maps implemented functions to ITU pipeline and readiness requirements.

80
00:06:31,450 --> 00:06:35,724
MedVerse links observations and policy evidence to institution-owned actions.

81
00:06:35,878 --> 00:06:38,356
Clinical and legal review remain pending.

82
00:06:38,510 --> 00:06:41,167
Coordinate the workflow. Preserve the authority.

83
00:06:41,321 --> 00:06:44,500
Learning proposes; policy architecture disposes.
